Susan Zhang (@suchenzang) comments that how a $125 billion market cap company has come to thoroughly enjoy the smell of their own AI garbage must be studied.

@sonyxperia: "The new AI Camera Assistant with Xperia Intelligence brings stories to life. Using subject, scene, and weather, it suggests expressive options with adjustments of colour, exposure, bokeh, and lens for breathtaking photos."

The feature aims to enhance photography by automatically suggesting adjustments based on scene analysis.

When you are a warlord presiding over tens of thousands, a stick is much more effective than looking for useful carrots already in your basket.

@EcZachly shares that Meta reached out to interview him for a principal role the same week they laid off 8,000 employees. He believes at least one of those let go would have been a good fit for the role. Instead, the company chose to axe everyone, treat them as a cost, and rehire only where there is pain.

He questions what happened to employee retention and criticizes how big tech companies expect loyalty without trying to retain staff, despite having hundreds of billions of dollars. Retaining one employee would be cheaper than layoffs, both financially and emotionally. The culture around AI and layoffs has become unbelievably toxic.

